body:
@garykac KeyboardLock looks like a great api, I look forward to seeing it progress - I don't think it aligns with the use cases I am hoping to address though. We don't always want to _lock_ the default behaviour, depending on the pattern - in fact often quite the opposite.

I'm hoping that for non-fullscreen webapps (think GitHub, gmail, chat apps, electron apps) which bind shortcuts for actions on those apps we can have a mechanism for detecting if a shortcut is _actionable_ without interrupting/disabling the user's expected action. Three concrete examples come to mind:

1. GitHub uses keys like `g n` to **g**o to the **n**otifications page. Of course, pressing `gn` inside of a text box should type the characters `gn` and not redirect the user to the notifications page. In this scenario we need to _let the default thing happen, and not act in any custom ways_ - in other words:
   ```js
   withTheKeys('g n', event => {
     if (userWantsToInputThisText(event)) {
       return
     } else {
        doOurCustomBehaviour() 
     }
   })
   ```

2. GitHub uses the key-combo `ctrl/cmd+shift+p` in a comment box to toggle between "Preview" and "Write" mode. This works great in all browsers - except in Firefox where this shortcut means "Open a new private browsing session". In this scenario we want to _let the default thing happen, and use our reporting tools to signal to us that our behaviour conflicts with native so we can fix it_. In other words 
   ```js
   withTheKeys('ctrl+shift+p', event => {
     if(thisShortcutConflictsWithABrowserShortcut(event)) {
       reportAndDoNothing()
     } else {
       doOurCustomBehaviour()
     }
   })
   ```

3. When in the file editor in GitHub (for example editing a README.md) we override the `Ctrl+F` behaviour, for Finding text. We _know_ we can offer a better user experience than the browsers default behaviour, as our find functionality is scoped to the editor pane and we can do more interesting things here. In this scenario, we want to _override the default behaviour, and provide our enhanced behaviour_. In other words
   ```js
   withTheKeys('ctrl+f', event => {
     if(thisShortcutConflictsWithABrowserShortcut(event)) {
       event.preventDefault()
       itsOkayWeKnowWhatWeAreDoing()
     }
   })
   ```
